1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,601
On the day before Christmas Eve, 1991, a man ran out of a burning house in

2
00:00:04,601 --> 00:00:09,700
Corsicana, Texas, and collapsed in the front yard, screaming that his three little

3
00:00:09,700 --> 00:00:11,192
girls were still inside.

4
00:00:11,492 --> 00:00:13,860
Neighbors had to hold him back from the flames.

5
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:19,307
Twelve years later, the State of Texas strapped that same man to a gurney and killed

6
00:00:19,307 --> 00:00:22,984
him — for setting that fire, for murdering his own children.

7
00:00:23,284 --> 00:00:28,079
And then, after he was already dead, a scientist the state itself had hired read the

8
00:00:28,079 --> 00:00:32,760
evidence one more time and reached a conclusion no one could take back: that there

9
00:00:32,760 --> 00:00:34,987
had probably never been a crime at all.

10
00:00:36,787 --> 00:00:38,970
His name was Cameron Todd Willingham.

11
00:00:39,270 --> 00:00:43,424
He was twenty-three years old when his daughters died, and he would spend the last

12
00:00:43,424 --> 00:00:46,515
twelve years of his life insisting he had tried to save them.

13
00:00:46,815 --> 00:00:48,440
His town didn't believe him.

14
00:00:48,740 --> 00:00:50,273
The jury didn't believe him.

15
00:00:50,573 --> 00:00:52,198
The governor didn't believe him.

16
00:00:52,498 --> 00:00:57,242
Everyone who mattered was certain — and the science that made them certain would,

17
00:00:57,242 --> 00:01:00,346
one careful reading at a time, come completely apart.

18
00:01:00,646 --> 00:01:02,132
Just never in time.

19
00:01:02,432 --> 00:01:06,837
Hold on to that gap between the truth and the timing, because everything that is

20
00:01:06,837 --> 00:01:09,259
unbearable about this story lives inside it.

21
00:01:11,059 --> 00:01:14,217
Start with the fire, because everyone else did.

22
00:01:14,517 --> 00:01:17,675
It was the morning of December 23rd, 1991.

23
00:01:17,975 --> 00:01:22,513
Todd Willingham was asleep in the little frame house on West 11th Street when his

24
00:01:22,513 --> 00:01:26,659
two-year-old daughter, Amber, woke him — the house was filling with smoke.

25
00:01:26,959 --> 00:01:30,860
His wife, Stacy, was out; she'd gone to pick up Christmas presents.

26
00:01:31,160 --> 00:01:36,333
So it was just Todd at home with Amber and her one-year-old twin sisters, Karmon and

27
00:01:36,333 --> 00:01:36,826
Kameron.

28
00:01:37,126 --> 00:01:41,459
What happened in the next few minutes would be dissected, frame by frame, for the

29
00:01:41,459 --> 00:01:43,117
rest of his life and beyond it.

30
00:01:43,417 --> 00:01:44,392
He got out.

31
00:01:44,692 --> 00:01:46,132
His daughters did not.

32
00:01:46,432 --> 00:01:51,850
By the time the fire trucks arrived, the house was roaring, and Todd was in the yard

33
00:01:51,850 --> 00:01:56,881
— burned in places, his hair singed, screaming for someone to save his babies.

34
00:01:57,181 --> 00:01:59,410
Neighbors described a man out of his mind.

35
00:01:59,710 --> 00:02:04,552
And here is where the story quietly forks, because some of those same neighbors, and

36
00:02:04,552 --> 00:02:07,837
soon the investigators, began to describe something else.

37
00:02:08,137 --> 00:02:10,041
That he seemed too calm at moments.

38
00:02:10,341 --> 00:02:14,613
That at one point he was worried about moving his car so it wouldn't catch fire.

39
00:02:14,913 --> 00:02:15,935
Small things.

40
00:02:16,235 --> 00:02:19,579
Human things, maybe — shock does strange things to people.

41
00:02:19,879 --> 00:02:24,337
But once a suspicion takes hold, every ordinary detail starts to look like evidence.

42
00:02:24,637 --> 00:02:26,866
And a suspicion did take hold.

43
00:02:27,166 --> 00:02:28,002
Fast.

44
00:02:28,302 --> 00:02:32,432
Because when the fire was out and the investigators walked through the blackened

45
00:02:32,432 --> 00:02:36,562
shell of the house, they believed they could read the fire like a language — and

46
00:02:36,562 --> 00:02:40,330
they believed it was telling them that this fire had been set on purpose.

47
00:02:40,630 --> 00:02:44,935
Hold that thought, because that "language" is the hinge this entire case turns on,

48
00:02:44,935 --> 00:02:49,082
and thirty years later it would be exposed as something closer to superstition.

49
00:02:49,382 --> 00:02:52,354
But in 1991, in Corsicana, it was gospel.

50
00:02:52,654 --> 00:02:57,212
Now the town went looking for the kind of man who could do such a thing — and Todd

51
00:02:57,212 --> 00:02:59,992
Willingham was an easy man to make into a monster.

52
00:03:00,292 --> 00:03:03,961
He was a high-school dropout with a temper and a record of trouble.

53
00:03:04,261 --> 00:03:05,143
He drank.

54
00:03:05,443 --> 00:03:07,997
There were allegations he'd been rough with Stacy.

55
00:03:08,297 --> 00:03:13,338
On his walls hung heavy-metal posters — Iron Maiden, Led Zeppelin, skulls and

56
00:03:13,338 --> 00:03:18,706
imagery that, to a frightened community in the grip of a satanic-panic era, looked

57
00:03:18,706 --> 00:03:20,604
like proof of something evil.

58
00:03:20,904 --> 00:03:23,226
He had tattoos of a skull and a serpent.

59
00:03:23,526 --> 00:03:25,012
None of that is a crime.

60
00:03:25,312 --> 00:03:26,752
None of that lights a fire.

61
00:03:27,052 --> 00:03:32,604
But stacked together and lit by grief and rage, it built a picture, and the picture

62
00:03:32,604 --> 00:03:34,343
was: this is not a victim.

63
00:03:34,643 --> 00:03:38,395
This is a killer who murdered his own children and then stood in the yard and

64
00:03:38,395 --> 00:03:40,587
performed the part of the heartbroken father.

65
00:03:40,887 --> 00:03:42,745
Even the grief was turned against him.

66
00:03:43,045 --> 00:03:46,875
At the girls' funeral, some mourners watched him and decided they were seeing a

67
00:03:46,875 --> 00:03:47,457
performance.

68
00:03:47,757 --> 00:03:52,113
A refrigerator in the kitchen had come to rest near a doorway, and investigators

69
00:03:52,113 --> 00:03:56,632
decided he had shoved it there to block his children's escape — though there was an

70
00:03:56,632 --> 00:03:58,810
ordinary explanation for where it stood.

71
00:03:59,110 --> 00:04:03,197
Stacy, for a time, stood by her husband and said she believed he was innocent.

72
00:04:03,497 --> 00:04:05,308
None of it slowed the machine down.

73
00:04:05,608 --> 00:04:10,031
In a town this size, burying three babies three days before Christmas, people did

74
00:04:10,031 --> 00:04:11,506
not want a tragic accident.

75
00:04:11,806 --> 00:04:13,246
They wanted someone to hate.

76
00:04:13,546 --> 00:04:19,105
And Todd Willingham — with his record, his temper, and his skulls on the wall — was

77
00:04:19,105 --> 00:04:20,512
standing right there.

78
00:04:20,812 --> 00:04:22,716
That was the story the town told.

79
00:04:23,016 --> 00:04:25,431
That was the story the state would tell a jury.

80
00:04:25,731 --> 00:04:30,884
And once you've decided a man is a monster, you stop asking whether the science that

81
00:04:30,884 --> 00:04:32,418
convicts him is any good.

82
00:04:32,718 --> 00:04:34,947
You just reach for it, and you use it.

83
00:04:35,247 --> 00:04:36,826
So let's look at the science.

84
00:04:37,126 --> 00:04:41,518
Because that is where this stops being one man's tragedy and becomes something that

85
00:04:41,518 --> 00:04:43,581
should frighten every single one of us.

86
00:04:45,381 --> 00:04:48,910
The case against Cameron Todd Willingham rested on two pillars.

87
00:04:49,210 --> 00:04:50,650
The first was fire.

88
00:04:50,950 --> 00:04:56,000
The lead investigators were a Texas deputy state fire marshal named Manuel Vasquez

89
00:04:56,000 --> 00:04:59,449
and a Corsicana assistant fire chief named Douglas Fogg.

90
00:04:59,749 --> 00:05:04,790
They walked the ruins, and they came out with a long list — roughly twenty separate

91
00:05:04,790 --> 00:05:07,644
"indicators," they said, that pointed to arson.

92
00:05:07,944 --> 00:05:10,730
To a jury, twenty of anything sounds overwhelming.

93
00:05:11,030 --> 00:05:15,110
So let me give you the ones that did the damage, because you need to see how

94
00:05:15,110 --> 00:05:18,600
convincing they sounded, and then how completely they would fall.

95
00:05:18,900 --> 00:05:23,262
They pointed to burn patterns on the floor — dark, puddle-shaped scorches that they

96
00:05:23,262 --> 00:05:27,677
said could only be the footprints of a liquid accelerant, poured out and set alight.

97
00:05:27,977 --> 00:05:29,742
They called them pour patterns.

98
00:05:30,042 --> 00:05:34,883
They pointed to "crazed glass," a spider-web of tiny cracks in the windows, which

99
00:05:34,883 --> 00:05:39,903
they testified was the signature of a fire that had grown unnaturally hot and fast —

100
00:05:39,903 --> 00:05:43,370
the kind of heat you only get when someone helps it along.

101
00:05:43,670 --> 00:05:48,495
They pointed to multiple, separate points where the fire seemed to have started, as

102
00:05:48,495 --> 00:05:51,286
if someone had lit it in several places at once.

103
00:05:51,586 --> 00:05:56,161
They pointed to burning low, along the floor and under the front door's threshold,

104
00:05:56,161 --> 00:05:59,341
where an honest accidental fire, they said, would not go.

105
00:05:59,641 --> 00:06:02,567
And they found a trace of accelerant near the front doorway.

106
00:06:02,867 --> 00:06:07,847
Put it together the way the prosecution did, and it was damning: a man pours a trail

107
00:06:07,847 --> 00:06:12,590
of liquid fire through his own house, lights it in several places, and walks out

108
00:06:12,590 --> 00:06:14,013
while his children burn.

109
00:06:14,313 --> 00:06:16,589
Vasquez was blunt about it on the stand.

110
00:06:16,889 --> 00:06:21,208
The fire, he testified, told him the story — and the story was murder.

111
00:06:21,508 --> 00:06:24,434
Then came the second pillar: a man named Johnny Webb.

112
00:06:24,734 --> 00:06:29,227
Webb was in the Navarro County jail on his own charges when he came forward to say

113
00:06:29,227 --> 00:06:33,775
that Willingham had confessed to him — that Todd had admitted setting the fire, and

114
00:06:33,775 --> 00:06:37,226
had said something monstrous about the children to cover it up.

115
00:06:37,526 --> 00:06:41,796
A jailhouse informant is one of the most powerful things you can put in front of a

116
00:06:41,796 --> 00:06:44,399
jury and, historically, one of the most dangerous.

117
00:06:44,699 --> 00:06:49,935
Because informants often want something — a reduced sentence, a favor, a way out —

118
00:06:49,935 --> 00:06:54,405
and juries rarely get to weigh how much that wanting shapes the words.

119
00:06:54,705 --> 00:06:59,813
What the jury in 1992 did not fully see was how much Johnny Webb had to gain.

120
00:07:00,113 --> 00:07:05,160
Keep Webb in the back of your mind, because his story does not stay put — years

121
00:07:05,160 --> 00:07:10,334
later it would twist, and recant, and twist again, and what it revealed about how

122
00:07:10,334 --> 00:07:13,720
this case was built would be its own kind of scandal.

123
00:07:14,020 --> 00:07:18,334
There was one more fact the jury never got to weigh, and it may be the most human

124
00:07:18,334 --> 00:07:19,825
detail in the entire record.

125
00:07:20,125 --> 00:07:25,163
Before trial, prosecutors offered Cameron Todd Willingham a deal: plead guilty, and

126
00:07:25,163 --> 00:07:27,045
instead of death, you get life.

127
00:07:27,345 --> 00:07:28,738
Take the plea and you live.

128
00:07:29,038 --> 00:07:30,153
He said no.

129
00:07:30,453 --> 00:07:35,092
A guilty man, staring down a needle, is handed a way to keep breathing — and he

130
00:07:35,092 --> 00:07:39,555
refuses it, because to take it he would have to say he killed his daughters.

131
00:07:39,855 --> 00:07:41,202
He would not say it.

132
00:07:41,502 --> 00:07:46,247
He turned down his own life to avoid confessing to a crime he insisted he did not

133
00:07:46,247 --> 00:07:46,657
commit.

134
00:07:46,957 --> 00:07:51,183
Sit with how strange that is if he was truly the monster the state described.

135
00:07:51,483 --> 00:07:56,339
There was one more voice at that trial, and it may be the most chilling — not for

136
00:07:56,339 --> 00:07:59,517
what it proved, but for what it was allowed to claim.

137
00:07:59,817 --> 00:08:04,274
To send a man to death row in Texas, a jury has to decide he will be dangerous in

138
00:08:04,274 --> 00:08:04,879
the future.

139
00:08:05,179 --> 00:08:09,965
So the state called a psychiatrist, who told the jury that Cameron Todd Willingham

140
00:08:09,965 --> 00:08:14,809
was a deep, remorseless sociopath — the kind of man who would kill again the moment

141
00:08:14,809 --> 00:08:15,860
he got the chance.

142
00:08:16,160 --> 00:08:20,756
And as proof of that darkness inside him, the prosecution again pointed to the

143
00:08:20,756 --> 00:08:23,172
heavy-metal posters and the skull tattoo.

144
00:08:23,472 --> 00:08:28,380
It was a confident, scientific-sounding verdict on the contents of a man's soul,

145
00:08:28,380 --> 00:08:33,043
delivered by a psychiatrist so notorious for exactly this brand of courtroom

146
00:08:33,043 --> 00:08:36,847
prophecy that his own profession would later expel him for it.

147
00:08:37,147 --> 00:08:39,376
One junk science said the fire was murder.

148
00:08:39,676 --> 00:08:42,509
A second junk science said the man was born to kill.

149
00:08:42,809 --> 00:08:45,735
Between the two of them, they built a death sentence.

150
00:08:46,035 --> 00:08:47,150
It didn't matter.

151
00:08:47,450 --> 00:08:50,701
In August of 1992, the jury took very little time.

152
00:08:51,001 --> 00:08:54,856
They convicted him of capital murder, and they sentenced him to death.

153
00:08:55,156 --> 00:09:00,032
The fire had spoken, an informant had sworn, and a monster had been named.

154
00:09:00,332 --> 00:09:04,790
Cameron Todd Willingham was sent to death row to wait for the state to kill him.

155
00:09:05,090 --> 00:09:08,666
And for a while, that is exactly where this story looked like it would end.

156
00:09:08,966 --> 00:09:10,173
A closed case.

157
00:09:10,473 --> 00:09:11,588
A guilty man.

158
00:09:11,888 --> 00:09:13,513
Justice, apparently, done.

159
00:09:13,813 --> 00:09:18,708
Except one person on the outside couldn't let it go — and what she found would turn

160
00:09:18,708 --> 00:09:23,426
the fire science of this case, and then the entire system behind it, inside out.

161
00:09:25,226 --> 00:09:26,944
Her name was Elizabeth Gilbert.

162
00:09:27,244 --> 00:09:32,135
She was a playwright, not an investigator, and she started writing to death-row

163
00:09:32,135 --> 00:09:33,931
prisoners almost by accident.

164
00:09:34,231 --> 00:09:36,135
One of them was Todd Willingham.

165
00:09:36,435 --> 00:09:37,875
She expected a monster.

166
00:09:38,175 --> 00:09:43,062
What she found, over months of letters and visits, was a man who talked about his

167
00:09:43,062 --> 00:09:45,838
kids, who didn't add up to the story on paper.

168
00:09:46,138 --> 00:09:50,638
So she started digging — pulling records, tracking down witnesses, asking the

169
00:09:50,638 --> 00:09:54,962
questions no one had bothered to ask once the town had decided who he was.

170
00:09:55,262 --> 00:10:00,048
And the deeper she went, the more the case felt less like a solved murder and more

171
00:10:00,048 --> 00:10:01,624
like a house built on sand.

172
00:10:01,924 --> 00:10:04,107
She was not a lawyer or a detective.

173
00:10:04,407 --> 00:10:08,865
She had no client, no career riding on the answer, nothing to win.

174
00:10:09,165 --> 00:10:13,483
That is part of what makes what she found so damning — it took an outsider with

175
00:10:13,483 --> 00:10:17,910
nothing to gain to notice that the emperor of this case was wearing no clothes at

176
00:10:17,910 --> 00:10:18,128
all.

177
00:10:18,428 --> 00:10:20,750
She couldn't crack the fire science herself.

178
00:10:21,050 --> 00:10:26,880
So, as the execution date closed in, the case reached a man who could — a chemist

179
00:10:26,880 --> 00:10:28,248
named Gerald Hurst.

180
00:10:28,548 --> 00:10:30,266
Hurst was not a soft touch.

181
00:10:30,566 --> 00:10:35,591
He was a Cambridge-trained scientist, a genuine expert in fire and explosives, and

182
00:10:35,591 --> 00:10:40,493
he had a reputation for going where the evidence went, no matter whose side that

183
00:10:40,493 --> 00:10:40,922
helped.

184
00:10:41,222 --> 00:10:46,569
He took the file, and he did the one thing no one had done: he tested every single

185
00:10:46,569 --> 00:10:50,417
"indicator" against what modern fire science actually knew.

186
00:10:50,717 --> 00:10:53,782
And what he found should have stopped the execution cold.

187
00:10:54,082 --> 00:10:58,958
Here is the reversal — the heart of this entire story, so let me make it plain.

188
00:10:59,258 --> 00:11:03,795
The "pour patterns" on the floor — the puddle-shaped burns the state swore could

189
00:11:03,795 --> 00:11:05,667
only come from poured accelerant?

190
00:11:05,967 --> 00:11:09,868
By the 1990s, scientists understood a phenomenon called flashover.

191
00:11:10,168 --> 00:11:15,814
In a closed room, a fire fills the space with superheated gas near the ceiling until

192
00:11:15,814 --> 00:11:21,057
the whole room reaches a temperature where everything ignites at once — floor,

193
00:11:21,057 --> 00:11:22,939
furniture, walls, all of it.

194
00:11:23,239 --> 00:11:27,745
After a flashover, the fire itself scorches the floor in exactly the pooled,

195
00:11:27,745 --> 00:11:32,666
irregular patterns that old investigators had spent decades calling proof of arson.

196
00:11:32,966 --> 00:11:35,752
The pour patterns weren't evidence of a poured liquid.

197
00:11:36,052 --> 00:11:40,139
They were the fingerprints of an ordinary fire that had gotten big enough.

198
00:11:40,439 --> 00:11:44,990
The "crazed glass," the spider-web cracks that supposedly proved unnatural heat?

199
00:11:45,290 --> 00:11:48,959
Scientists had shown that crazing isn't caused by fast heat at all.

200
00:11:49,259 --> 00:11:53,511
It's caused by cold water hitting hot glass — which is to say, it's caused by

201
00:11:53,511 --> 00:11:55,389
firefighters spraying their hoses.

202
00:11:55,689 --> 00:12:00,156
The very act of putting the fire out created the "evidence" that a fire had been

203
00:12:00,156 --> 00:12:00,379
set.

204
00:12:00,679 --> 00:12:06,309
The multiple points of origin, the low burning, the char under the threshold — one

205
00:12:06,309 --> 00:12:10,292
by one, flashover and normal fire behavior explained them.

206
00:12:10,592 --> 00:12:15,368
Even the trace of accelerant by the front door had an innocent answer: there was a

207
00:12:15,368 --> 00:12:17,930
small grill and lighter fluid on that porch.

208
00:12:18,230 --> 00:12:23,335
Every pillar of the fire case, the entire twenty-indicator tower that had sent a man

209
00:12:23,335 --> 00:12:28,075
to death row, was built on folklore that real science had already left behind.

210
00:12:28,375 --> 00:12:32,935
Hurst's conclusion was devastating in its simplicity: there was no valid evidence

211
00:12:32,935 --> 00:12:34,737
that this fire was arson at all.

212
00:12:35,037 --> 00:12:37,545
It looked, for all the world, like an accident.

213
00:12:37,845 --> 00:12:39,378
Think about what that means.

214
00:12:39,678 --> 00:12:43,904
If the fire wasn't arson, then no one murdered those three little girls.

215
00:12:44,204 --> 00:12:46,108
There was no killer in that house.

216
00:12:46,408 --> 00:12:50,695
There was only a father who couldn't reach his children in time — which is exactly

217
00:12:50,695 --> 00:12:54,303
what he had said, screaming, in the yard, from the very first minute.

218
00:12:54,603 --> 00:12:55,671
Hurst wrote it up.

219
00:12:55,971 --> 00:13:00,569
And in the days before the execution, his report was sent to the people with the

220
00:13:00,569 --> 00:13:05,167
power to stop it — the Texas Board of Pardons and Paroles, and the office of the

221
00:13:05,167 --> 00:13:06,374
governor, Rick Perry.

222
00:13:06,674 --> 00:13:11,479
A working scientist was telling the State of Texas, in writing, before it was too

223
00:13:11,479 --> 00:13:14,801
late: the science you used to condemn this man is wrong.

224
00:13:15,101 --> 00:13:17,191
And the State of Texas killed him anyway.

225
00:13:17,491 --> 00:13:18,791
There was no stay.

226
00:13:19,091 --> 00:13:20,577
Clemency was denied.

227
00:13:20,877 --> 00:13:26,638
On February 17th, 2004, Cameron Todd Willingham was put to death by lethal injection

228
00:13:26,638 --> 00:13:28,215
in the Huntsville Unit.

229
00:13:28,515 --> 00:13:33,169
He went to his death the same way he had lived the last twelve years of his life —

230
00:13:33,169 --> 00:13:34,645
insisting he was innocent.

231
00:13:34,945 --> 00:13:36,663
He was thirty-six years old.

232
00:13:36,963 --> 00:13:39,610
And it was not for lack of chances to stop it.

233
00:13:39,910 --> 00:13:44,268
His appeals had climbed through the courts for years, and by the end the Hurst

234
00:13:44,268 --> 00:13:48,849
report was sitting right there in the record — a working scientist's plain warning

235
00:13:48,849 --> 00:13:50,916
that the fire evidence was worthless.

236
00:13:51,216 --> 00:13:54,002
Court after court let the execution stand anyway.

237
00:13:54,302 --> 00:13:58,370
Some never squarely wrestled with the new science; others simply had no more

238
00:13:58,370 --> 00:14:00,618
patience for a man already stamped guilty.

239
00:14:00,918 --> 00:14:05,562
The system had a dozen doors marked exit, and it walked past every one of them.

240
00:14:05,862 --> 00:14:10,425
Whatever else is uncertain in this case, this is not: the warning arrived, in time,

241
00:14:10,425 --> 00:14:14,878
from a qualified scientist, and the machinery of the state rolled forward over it

242
00:14:14,878 --> 00:14:16,032
without slowing down.

243
00:14:16,332 --> 00:14:20,790
Now, if the story ended there, you could call it a tragedy and look away.

244
00:14:21,090 --> 00:14:26,010
A last-minute report, brushed aside in the rush of an execution — terrible, but

245
00:14:26,010 --> 00:14:26,570
arguable.

246
00:14:26,870 --> 00:14:31,421
The reason this case will not let anyone look away is what happened next.

247
00:14:31,721 --> 00:14:36,337
Because after Todd Willingham was dead and buried, the State of Texas accidentally

248
00:14:36,337 --> 00:14:37,294
proved him right.

249
00:14:37,594 --> 00:14:43,343
Texas had created something called the Forensic Science Commission — a body meant to

250
00:14:43,343 --> 00:14:47,997
investigate exactly this kind of question: was the science reliable?

251
00:14:48,297 --> 00:14:52,811
The commission took up the Willingham case, and it hired one of the country's

252
00:14:52,811 --> 00:14:57,090
leading fire scientists, a man named Craig Beyler, to review the original

253
00:14:57,090 --> 00:14:59,025
investigation from top to bottom.

254
00:14:59,325 --> 00:15:02,669
Beyler's report landed in 2009, and it was not gentle.

255
00:15:02,969 --> 00:15:07,455
He found that the arson finding could not be supported by the evidence — that, in

256
00:15:07,455 --> 00:15:10,446
his words, "a finding of arson could not be sustained.

257
00:15:10,746 --> 00:15:14,709
And then he went further, and aimed a sentence directly at the methods that had

258
00:15:14,709 --> 00:15:15,762
convicted Willingham.

259
00:15:16,062 --> 00:15:21,081
The fire marshal's testimony, Beyler wrote, was "hardly consistent with a scientific

260
00:15:21,081 --> 00:15:24,607
mind-set and is more characteristic of mystics or psychics.

261
00:15:24,907 --> 00:15:29,140
A state-commissioned expert had just described the evidence that killed a man as

262
00:15:29,140 --> 00:15:31,362
closer to fortune-telling than to science.

263
00:15:31,662 --> 00:15:33,845
That should have been a reckoning.

264
00:15:34,145 --> 00:15:36,699
Instead, it became a cover-up in slow motion.

265
00:15:36,999 --> 00:15:41,026
Just two days before the commission was scheduled to meet and discuss Beyler's

266
00:15:41,026 --> 00:15:45,363
findings, Governor Perry replaced three of its members — including its chairman, Sam

267
00:15:45,363 --> 00:15:45,776
Bassett.

268
00:15:46,076 --> 00:15:49,234
His new appointee took over as chair and canceled the meeting.

269
00:15:49,534 --> 00:15:53,992
The review that might have officially branded a Texas execution as a mistake was

270
00:15:53,992 --> 00:15:55,664
stopped before it could speak.

271
00:15:55,964 --> 00:16:00,554
Perry, who was running for re-election, denied interfering, and publicly stuck to

272
00:16:00,554 --> 00:16:02,141
the town's original verdict.

273
00:16:02,441 --> 00:16:04,577
Willingham, he said, was "a monster."

274
00:16:04,877 --> 00:16:07,106
But the science would not be un-said.

275
00:16:07,406 --> 00:16:11,566
The Innocence Project brought in five of the nation's top fire experts to look at

276
00:16:11,566 --> 00:16:12,747
the case independently.

277
00:16:13,047 --> 00:16:17,820
Their report ran forty-eight pages, and its conclusion left no room to hide: "none

278
00:16:17,820 --> 00:16:21,778
of the scientific analysis used to convict Mr. Willingham was valid.

279
00:16:22,078 --> 00:16:22,960
Not weak.

280
00:16:23,260 --> 00:16:24,375
Not outdated.

281
00:16:24,675 --> 00:16:25,743
Not valid.

282
00:16:26,043 --> 00:16:27,111
Zero of it.

283
00:16:27,411 --> 00:16:31,823
And the second pillar — the informant — was crumbling too.

284
00:16:32,123 --> 00:16:36,620
Johnny Webb, the man who'd sworn Willingham confessed in jail, took his story back,

285
00:16:36,620 --> 00:16:40,575
and it came out that he had gotten favorable treatment for his testimony.

286
00:16:40,875 --> 00:16:46,095
His account had wobbled for years — recanted, then partly walked back — but the

287
00:16:46,095 --> 00:16:51,315
pattern underneath it was clear enough: the confession that helped seal a death

288
00:16:51,315 --> 00:16:55,411
sentence rested on a witness who wanted something, and got it.

289
00:16:55,711 --> 00:16:59,612
The charge Webb had been facing was quietly reduced after he testified.

290
00:16:59,912 --> 00:17:04,314
Part of a man's death sentence, it turned out, had been traded for another man's

291
00:17:04,314 --> 00:17:04,974
lighter one.

292
00:17:05,274 --> 00:17:06,853
So look at what was left.

293
00:17:07,153 --> 00:17:08,546
The fire: not arson.

294
00:17:08,846 --> 00:17:13,165
The confession: from an informant who was compensated and later recanted.

295
00:17:13,465 --> 00:17:17,459
Two pillars, both fallen — after the execution, not before.

296
00:17:19,259 --> 00:17:23,446
Here is where an honest story has to slow down and tell you the truth about the

297
00:17:23,446 --> 00:17:23,764
truth.

298
00:17:24,064 --> 00:17:27,222
Cameron Todd Willingham has never been exonerated.

299
00:17:27,522 --> 00:17:29,658
No court has thrown out his conviction.

300
00:17:29,958 --> 00:17:35,320
There is no official document, anywhere, that says the State of Texas executed an

301
00:17:35,320 --> 00:17:36,181
innocent man.

302
00:17:36,481 --> 00:17:40,289
His ex-wife, Stacy, at one point said she believed he was guilty.

303
00:17:40,589 --> 00:17:45,745
The case is, to this day, formally contested, and the people who prosecuted him have

304
00:17:45,745 --> 00:17:47,648
never conceded they were wrong.

305
00:17:47,948 --> 00:17:52,618
If you came here wanting a clean verdict — a moment where a judge bangs a gavel and

306
00:17:52,618 --> 00:17:56,725
declares him innocent — it does not exist, and I won't invent it for you.

307
00:17:57,025 --> 00:17:59,161
But that is not the same as not knowing.

308
00:17:59,461 --> 00:18:00,761
What we know is this.

309
00:18:01,061 --> 00:18:05,375
The fire science that convicted him has been examined by some of the best fire

310
00:18:05,375 --> 00:18:09,467
scientists in the world — before his death, and after it — and they agree.

311
00:18:09,767 --> 00:18:14,787
The pour patterns, the crazed glass, the points of origin, the whole twenty-item

312
00:18:14,787 --> 00:18:15,665
case: invalid.

313
00:18:15,965 --> 00:18:19,216
A state-commissioned expert compared it to the work of psychics.

314
00:18:19,516 --> 00:18:22,210
Five independent experts said none of it held.

315
00:18:22,510 --> 00:18:26,766
The single most likely explanation for what happened in that house on West 11th

316
00:18:26,766 --> 00:18:27,897
Street is not murder.

317
00:18:28,197 --> 00:18:32,423
It's an accidental fire, and a father who couldn't get to his kids.

318
00:18:32,723 --> 00:18:37,238
There is a name for what nearly everyone who has studied the evidence now believes

319
00:18:37,238 --> 00:18:41,643
happened in that house, and it is the most ordinary phrase in the world: a house

320
00:18:41,643 --> 00:18:41,918
fire.

321
00:18:42,218 --> 00:18:47,806
A space heater, faulty wiring, a small child with a match — no one ever had to prove

322
00:18:47,806 --> 00:18:53,061
which, because for a fire in a wood-frame house full of little kids, the honest

323
00:18:53,061 --> 00:18:54,525
default is not murder.

324
00:18:54,825 --> 00:18:57,983
It's an accident, until real evidence says otherwise.

325
00:18:58,283 --> 00:18:59,955
And real evidence never did.

326
00:19:00,255 --> 00:19:03,877
Which means the real horror of this case isn't that the system got a hard call

327
00:19:03,877 --> 00:19:04,156
wrong.

328
00:19:04,456 --> 00:19:09,892
It's that the system was warned, in writing, by a qualified scientist, while there

329
00:19:09,892 --> 00:19:12,676
was still time — and it killed him anyway.

330
00:19:12,976 --> 00:19:17,616
And then, when the proof kept coming, it looked away, reassigned the people asking

331
00:19:17,616 --> 00:19:22,312
the questions, and called the dead man a monster so it would never have to call him

332
00:19:22,312 --> 00:19:22,821
a victim.

333
00:19:23,121 --> 00:19:27,647
We built a machine powerful enough to end a human life, and we fed it evidence that

334
00:19:27,647 --> 00:19:29,065
turned out to be folklore.

335
00:19:29,365 --> 00:19:33,516
The tragedy of Cameron Todd Willingham is not only that he may have died for a crime

336
00:19:33,516 --> 00:19:34,752
that was never committed.

337
00:19:35,052 --> 00:19:39,832
It's that everything we needed to know, we could have known in time — and we chose

338
00:19:39,832 --> 00:19:43,504
the certainty of a monster over the doubt of a grieving father.

339
00:19:43,804 --> 00:19:48,109
He said it from the first minute in the yard to the last minute on the gurney: he

340
00:19:48,109 --> 00:19:48,959
did not do this.

341
00:19:49,259 --> 00:19:53,583
The science we have now says he may well have been telling the truth the entire

342
00:19:53,583 --> 00:19:53,857
time.

343
00:19:54,157 --> 00:19:57,129
Texas just couldn't hear it until the man was already gone.

344
00:19:57,429 --> 00:20:02,105
If you think a warning from a scientist should be enough to stop an execution —

345
00:20:02,105 --> 00:20:07,018
before it happens, not five years too late — hit like, so the next person who reads

346
00:20:07,018 --> 00:20:08,853
a fire learns to read it right.
